T("Grammar","Parallel structure and modifiers","Balanced lists and clear describing words","⚖️","",
 ["Parallel structure means items in a list use the same form: running, swimming and biking.", "A modifier should sit next to the word it describes. A misplaced modifier makes the meaning unclear or funny.", "A dangling modifier has nothing to describe. Fix it by naming who does the action."],
 ["Which sentence has parallel structure?|She likes hiking, swimming and reading.|She likes hiking, to swim and reading.|She likes to hike, swimming and reads.|She likes hiking, swim and to read.", "Which sentence has parallel structure?|He was tired, hungry and cold.|He was tired, hungry and felt cold.|He was tired, had hunger and cold.|He was tiring, hungry and a cold.", "Which sentence has parallel structure?|We came, we saw, we left.|We came, seeing and left.|We came, we saw and leaving.|Coming, we saw, we left.", "Which sentence has a misplaced modifier?|I saw a dog running down the street with a long tail.|I saw a dog with a long tail running down the street.|A dog with a long tail ran down the street.|Running down the street, the dog wagged its long tail.", "Which sentence has a dangling modifier?|After finishing the test, the bell rang.|After finishing the test, I heard the bell ring.|The bell rang after I finished the test.|I finished the test before the bell rang.", "Which sentence means that she ate most of the pizza?|She ate almost the whole pizza.|She almost ate the whole pizza.|Almost she ate the whole pizza.|She ate the whole almost pizza.|\"Almost ate\" would mean she did not eat it at all.", "Which is the best fix for \"Walking home, the rain soaked me\"?|Walking home, I was soaked by the rain.|Walking home, the rain was soaking.|The rain, walking home, soaked me.|Walking home the rain, soaked me.", "Which sentence has parallel structure?|The job needs skill, patience and honesty.|The job needs skill, being patient and honesty.|The job needs skilled, patience and to be honest.|The job needs skill, patience and you are honest.", "Where should \"only\" go to mean that Sam ate nothing else?|Sam ate only the salad.|Only Sam ate the salad.|Sam only ate the salad yesterday.|Sam ate the only salad.", "Which sentence has parallel structure?|Not only did she sing, but she also danced.|Not only did she sing, but also dancing.|Not only singing, but she also danced.|She not only sang, but also to dance."]);
T("Writing","Writing about literature","Point, proof and explanation","📝","",
 ["A strong paragraph makes a point, gives proof from the text and explains how the proof supports the point.", "Proof is usually a short quotation. Introduce it and put it in quotation marks.", "Write about literature in the present tense, and avoid saying I think."],
 ["What are the three parts of a strong analytical paragraph?|Point, proof, explanation|Title, summary, opinion|Question, answer, joke|Setting, plot, ending", "What should follow a quotation in a paragraph?|An explanation of what it shows|Another quotation right away|The author's birthday|A new topic", "Which tense is normally used to write about a story?|Present tense|Past tense|Future tense|Conditional tense", "Which sentence is best for a literary essay?|The author shows that fear controls the villagers.|I think the author is kind of saying fear is bad.|The book was really good and I liked it.|In this essay I will talk about fear.", "What is the difference between summary and analysis?|Summary retells, analysis explains meaning|Summary is longer than analysis|Analysis retells, summary explains meaning|There is no difference", "Which sentence introduces a quotation correctly?|The narrator admits, \"I was afraid of the dark.\"|\"I was afraid of the dark.\" This is a quote.|The narrator admits \"I was afraid of the dark\"|Quote: I was afraid of the dark.", "What is a thesis in a literary essay?|An arguable claim about the text|A fact everyone agrees on|The plot summary|A list of characters", "Which of these is an arguable thesis?|The storm in the novel reflects the hero's inner conflict.|The novel has twelve chapters.|The hero's name is Daniel.|The novel was published in 1990.", "What should each body paragraph begin with?|A topic sentence|A quotation|A question for the reader|The conclusion", "Why should you avoid long plot summary in an essay?|The reader needs your analysis, not a retelling|Summaries are always wrong|Teachers do not read summaries|Plot does not matter"]);
T("Literature","Characters and narrators","How writers build people and tell the story","🎭","",
 ["Direct characterization tells you what a character is like. Indirect characterization shows it through speech, thoughts, actions and what others say.", "A dynamic character changes during the story. A static character stays the same.", "A first-person narrator uses I. An omniscient narrator knows the thoughts of every character. A limited narrator follows one character."],
 ["\"Maria was generous.\" What kind of characterization is this?|Direct|Indirect|Dynamic|Static", "\"Maria gave her lunch to the new student.\" What kind of characterization is this?|Indirect|Direct|Flat|Omniscient", "What is a character who changes during the story called?|Dynamic|Static|Flat|Minor", "What is a character who stays the same called?|Static|Dynamic|Round|Tragic", "What is a character with many sides and a complex personality called?|Round|Flat|Stock|Static", "Which narrator knows the thoughts of all the characters?|Third person omniscient|First person|Third person limited|Second person", "Which narrator uses \"I\" and tells only what he or she knows?|First person|Third person omniscient|Third person limited|Second person", "What is a narrator whose account cannot be fully trusted called?|Unreliable narrator|Omniscient narrator|Static narrator|Minor narrator", "What is a scene that goes back to an earlier time called?|Flashback|Foreshadowing|Climax|Epilogue", "What is the reason a character acts in a certain way called?|Motivation|Setting|Dialogue|Resolution"]);
T("Literature","Meeting Shakespeare","Plays, language and stage terms","🎭","",
 ["William Shakespeare wrote plays and poems in England about 400 years ago. His plays are tragedies, comedies and histories.", "In a soliloquy a character speaks his or her thoughts aloud while alone. An aside is a short remark that other characters do not hear.", "Much of his verse is in iambic pentameter: ten syllables to a line, with every second one stressed."],
 ["In which country did Shakespeare live and write?|England|France|Italy|United States", "What is a speech in which a character alone on stage speaks his or her thoughts called?|Soliloquy|Aside|Dialogue|Prologue", "What is a short remark to the audience that other characters do not hear called?|Aside|Soliloquy|Monologue|Epilogue", "What is a play that ends with the downfall or death of the main character called?|Tragedy|Comedy|History|Farce", "How many syllables are usually in a line of iambic pentameter?|Ten|Eight|Twelve|Fourteen", "How many acts does a Shakespeare play usually have?|Five|Three|Two|Seven", "In Shakespeare's English, what does \"thou\" mean?|You|They|There|Then", "What is the weakness that leads to a tragic hero's downfall called?|Tragic flaw|Comic relief|Dramatic irony|Stage direction", "In which city is \"Romeo and Juliet\" set?|Verona|London|Paris|Athens", "What is a joke that plays on words with two meanings called?|Pun|Simile|Couplet|Aside"]);
T("Reading","Reading: The Halifax Explosion","Read the passage, then answer","⚓","Answer from the passage.",
 ["Read the whole passage first.", "Look back at the passage to find the answer. Do not guess from memory.", "An inference is something the passage suggests but does not state directly."],
 ["When did the explosion happen?|December 6, 1917|December 6, 1914|November 11, 1918|July 1, 1917", "What was the Mont-Blanc carrying?|Explosives|Passengers|Christmas trees|Coal only", "Why were crowds near the shore?|They had gathered to watch the burning ship|They were waiting for a train|They were boarding the ship|They were celebrating", "About how many people were killed?|Nearly two thousand|About two hundred|About nine thousand|About twenty thousand", "What was Vince Coleman's job?|Railway dispatcher|Ship's captain|Firefighter|Doctor", "What did Vince Coleman do?|He warned an incoming train to stop|He steered the ship away|He put out the fire|He sailed to Boston", "What can you infer about Vince Coleman?|He put the safety of others before his own|He did not know the ship was dangerous|He wanted to leave but could not|He was a passenger on the train", "Which American city sent help?|Boston|New York|Chicago|Seattle", "Why does Nova Scotia send a Christmas tree to Boston every year?|To thank the city for its help|To sell trees in the United States|Because Boston has no trees|To celebrate the end of the war", "What is the main idea of the passage?|A terrible explosion struck Halifax, and people responded with courage and help|Ships should not carry explosives|Boston is a generous city|Telegraphs were important in 1917"],"","On the morning of December 6, 1917, two ships collided in the narrow harbour of Halifax, Nova Scotia. One of them, the Mont-Blanc, was loaded with explosives for the war in Europe. It caught fire and drifted toward the shore while crowds gathered to watch. Just after nine o'clock it exploded. The blast flattened much of the north end of the city, killed nearly two thousand people and injured about nine thousand more. A railway dispatcher named Vince Coleman stayed at his post to send a telegraph warning to an incoming passenger train. The train stopped in time, but Coleman died in the explosion. Help soon arrived from across Canada and from the American city of Boston. Every year Nova Scotia still sends Boston a Christmas tree to say thank you.");
T("Media","Media literacy","Who made this message, and why?","📺","",
 ["Every media message is made by someone for a purpose: to inform, to entertain or to persuade.", "The target audience is the group the message is aimed at. Makers choose words, images and music to appeal to that group.", "Ask what is left out. A stereotype is an oversimplified picture of a group of people."],
 ["What is the group of people a message is aimed at called?|Target audience|Focus team|Media owners|Sponsors", "What is the main purpose of most advertisements?|To persuade|To inform only|To entertain only|To warn", "What is an oversimplified image of a group of people called?|Stereotype|Symbol|Slogan|Logo", "What is a short, catchy phrase used in advertising called?|Slogan|Headline|Caption|Byline", "An article presents only one side of an issue. What does it show?|Bias|Balance|Satire|Fact-checking", "What is information spread on purpose to mislead people called?|Disinformation|Journalism|Editorial|Satire", "What is the best way to check a surprising claim you see online?|Look for the same claim in reliable sources|Share it right away|Count how many likes it has|Trust it if it has a photo", "What is paid content made to look like a normal article called?|Sponsored content|Breaking news|An editorial|A documentary", "A toy advertisement is shown during cartoons. Who is the target audience?|Children|Grandparents|Teachers|Business owners", "What does \"media messages are constructed\" mean?|Someone chose what to include and what to leave out|They are always false|They are built by machines|They cannot be changed"]);
