T("Grammar","Words that get confused","Affect and effect, fewer and less …","🔀","",
 ["Affect is usually a verb meaning to influence. Effect is usually a noun meaning a result.", "Use fewer for things you can count and less for things you cannot.", "Then is about time. Than is for comparing. Whose shows ownership. Who's means who is."],
 ["Choose the correct word: \"The weather will ___ our plans.\"|affect|effect|affects on|effected", "Choose the correct word: \"The medicine had a strong ___.\"|effect|affect|affecting|effects on", "Choose the correct word: \"There were ___ students in class today.\"|fewer|less|lesser|least", "Choose the correct word: \"Please use ___ water.\"|less|fewer|few|fewest", "Choose the correct word: \"She is taller ___ her brother.\"|than|then|that|them", "Choose the correct word: \"___ jacket is this?\"|Whose|Who's|Whos|Who", "Choose the correct words: \"I ___ gone earlier.\"|should have|should of|should off|shoulda of", "Choose the correct word: \"I need to ___ down for an hour.\"|lie|lay|laid|lain", "Choose the correct word: \"Please ___ the book on the table.\"|lay|lie|lain|lied", "Choose the correct word: \"Everyone came ___ Omar.\"|except|accept|expect|excerpt"]);
T("Writing","Research and sources","Finding, using and citing information","🔎","",
 ["A primary source comes from the time or the person being studied, such as a diary or an interview. A secondary source discusses it later, such as a textbook.", "Use a direct quotation for exact words and a paraphrase for an idea in your own words. Both need a citation.", "A list of all your sources goes at the end of the paper."],
 ["Which of these is a primary source?|A soldier's diary from the war|A textbook chapter about the war|A documentary made last year|An encyclopedia article", "Which of these is a secondary source?|A biography of a prime minister|A letter written by the prime minister|A photograph from the event|A speech recorded at the time", "What must you include when you use someone else's idea in your own words?|A citation|Nothing|Only quotation marks|A footnote saying \"my idea\"", "What must surround an author's exact words?|Quotation marks|Brackets|Italics only|Nothing", "What is the list of sources at the end of an essay called in MLA style?|Works Cited|Index|Table of contents|Glossary", "Which source is most likely to be reliable?|A peer-reviewed journal article|An anonymous blog post|A social media comment|An advertisement", "What should you check to judge whether a website is trustworthy?|The author, the date and the purpose|The colours and fonts|How long the page is|Whether it has videos", "What is presenting someone else's work as your own called?|Plagiarism|Citation|Paraphrase|Research", "In MLA style, what usually goes in brackets after a quotation?|The author's last name and page number|The title of your essay|Your teacher's name|The date you read it", "Why do writers cite their sources?|To give credit and let readers check the information|To make the essay longer|Because quotations are not allowed|To hide where ideas came from"]);
T("Writing","Rhetorical devices","Tools that make speeches and essays powerful","🎤","",
 ["Rhetorical devices are ways of using language to persuade or to make an idea memorable.", "Repetition at the start of sentences is called anaphora. Antithesis puts two opposite ideas side by side.", "An allusion is a brief reference to a well-known person, event or text. An analogy explains one thing by comparing it to another."],
 ["\"We shall fight on the beaches, we shall fight on the landing grounds.\" Which device is this?|Anaphora|Allusion|Oxymoron|Understatement", "\"It was the best of times, it was the worst of times.\" Which device is this?|Antithesis|Euphemism|Analogy|Hyperbole", "\"He was a real Romeo with the ladies.\" Which device is this?|Allusion|Antithesis|Anaphora|Understatement", "\"Deafening silence\" is an example of what?|Oxymoron|Allusion|Analogy|Anaphora", "Saying \"passed away\" instead of \"died\" is an example of what?|Euphemism|Hyperbole|Oxymoron|Antithesis", "After a hurricane, someone says, \"We had a bit of wind.\" Which device is this?|Understatement|Hyperbole|Allusion|Anaphora", "\"A good book is like a good friend: it stays with you.\" Which device is this?|Analogy|Oxymoron|Euphemism|Antithesis", "\"Who would not want a better future?\" Which device is this?|Rhetorical question|Allusion|Understatement|Antithesis", "\"Less is more\" seems to contradict itself but holds a truth. What is it?|Paradox|Euphemism|Anaphora|Analogy", "A speaker lists three things: \"government of the people, by the people, for the people.\" Which device is this?|Parallelism|Oxymoron|Understatement|Euphemism"]);
T("Literature","Irony, tone and theme","Reading beneath the surface","🎭","",
 ["Verbal irony is saying the opposite of what you mean. Situational irony is when the opposite of what is expected happens. Dramatic irony is when the audience knows something a character does not.", "Tone is the writer's attitude. Mood is the feeling the reader gets.", "A motif is an image or idea that repeats. Satire uses humour to criticize."],
 ["It is pouring rain and someone says, \"Lovely weather!\" What kind of irony is this?|Verbal irony|Dramatic irony|Situational irony|No irony", "The audience knows the villain is hiding in the closet, but the hero does not. What kind of irony is this?|Dramatic irony|Verbal irony|Situational irony|Satire", "A police station is robbed. What kind of irony is this?|Situational irony|Verbal irony|Dramatic irony|Allegory", "What is the writer's attitude toward the subject called?|Tone|Mood|Theme|Plot", "What is the feeling a text creates in the reader called?|Mood|Tone|Motif|Style", "What is an image or idea that repeats through a work called?|Motif|Climax|Stanza|Setting", "What is writing that uses humour to criticize people or society called?|Satire|Tragedy|Allegory|Epic", "What is a story in which characters and events stand for bigger ideas called?|Allegory|Satire|Memoir|Sonnet", "Which of these is a theme rather than a topic?|Power can corrupt even good people|Power|War|Friendship", "\"The dark, silent house seemed to be waiting.\" What mood does this create?|Suspenseful|Cheerful|Playful|Peaceful"]);
T("Reading","Reading: Viola Desmond","Read the passage, then answer","🎟","Answer from the passage.",
 ["Read the whole passage first.", "Look back at the passage to find the answer. Do not guess from memory.", "Ask why the writer included each detail."],
 ["Why did Viola Desmond stop in New Glasgow?|Her car broke down|She lived there|She owned the theatre|She was visiting family", "Where did she sit in the theatre?|On the main floor|In the balcony|In the lobby|In the front row of the balcony", "Why was she told to move?|The main floor was kept for white customers|She had no ticket at all|The film was sold out|She was talking loudly", "What did she do when told to move?|She refused|She moved to the balcony|She left the theatre|She asked for her money back", "What was she fined for?|Not paying a one-cent difference in tax|Sitting in the wrong seat|Refusing to leave|Damaging the theatre", "What does the sentence \"Race was never mentioned in court\" suggest?|The real reason for her arrest was hidden behind a tax charge|The court treated everyone the same|She was not treated unfairly|The tax was very important", "Did she win her court case?|No, she lost|Yes, she won|The case was dropped|She never went to court", "What happened in 2010?|Nova Scotia granted her a pardon|She appeared on the ten-dollar bill|She opened a new business|The theatre closed", "On which bill did her portrait appear in 2018?|The ten-dollar bill|The five-dollar bill|The twenty-dollar bill|The hundred-dollar bill", "What is the main idea of the passage?|One woman's refusal to accept unfair treatment helped the fight for equal rights|Theatres in 1946 charged different taxes|Cars often broke down in the 1940s|Courts always make fair decisions"],"","In November 1946, Viola Desmond, a Black businesswoman from Halifax, was driving through New Glasgow, Nova Scotia, when her car broke down. While it was being repaired, she went to see a film at the Roseland Theatre. She took a seat on the main floor. She did not know that the theatre kept that section for white customers and sent Black customers to the balcony. When she was told to move, she refused. Police dragged her out, and she spent the night in jail. The next day she was fined for failing to pay a one-cent difference in tax between the two kinds of ticket. Race was never mentioned in court. Desmond fought the charge and lost, but her case inspired others to work for equal rights. In 2010 the government of Nova Scotia granted her a pardon, and in 2018 her portrait appeared on the ten-dollar bill.");
T("Words","Academic words","Analyze, evaluate, infer …","🎓","",
 ["Test and essay questions use command words. Each one asks you to do something different.", "Analyze means break something into parts and explain how they work. Evaluate means judge how good or effective it is.", "To infer is to work out something that is not stated. To imply is to suggest it without saying it."],
 ["What does \"analyze\" ask you to do?|Break it into parts and explain how they work|Retell it briefly|Copy it out|Give only your opinion", "What does \"evaluate\" ask you to do?|Judge its worth or effectiveness|List its parts|Retell it in order|Define each word", "What does \"summarize\" ask you to do?|Give the main points briefly|Explain every detail|Argue against it|Compare it with another text", "What does \"compare\" ask you to do?|Show how things are alike|Show only how they differ|Choose the best one|Describe one thing", "What does \"contrast\" ask you to do?|Show how things are different|Show how they are alike|Put them in order|Summarize both", "What does \"justify\" ask you to do?|Give reasons or evidence for your answer|State your answer only|Ask a question|Rewrite the question", "What does \"infer\" mean?|Work out something not stated directly|State something clearly|Copy a quotation|Ignore the evidence", "A writer hints at an idea without stating it. What is the writer doing?|Implying|Inferring|Summarizing|Citing", "What does \"synthesize\" ask you to do?|Combine ideas from several sources into a new whole|Pick one source and ignore the rest|List sources in order|Translate a text", "What does \"cite\" mean?|Name the source of information|See something|Choose a place|Change the wording"]);

