T("Grammar","Advanced punctuation and usage","Dashes, hyphens, who and whom","✒️","",
 ["A dash sets off a strong break or an added thought. A hyphen joins words, as in well-known author.", "Use who for a subject and whom for an object. If you can answer with him, use whom.", "A comma splice wrongly joins two sentences with only a comma. Fix it with a period, a semicolon or a joining word."],
 ["Which sentence contains a comma splice?|The test was hard, nobody finished it.|The test was hard, and nobody finished it.|The test was hard; nobody finished it.|Because the test was hard, nobody finished it.", "Which sentence uses a hyphen correctly?|She is a well-known author.|She is a well known-author.|She is well-known as an author-.|She is a wellknown author.", "Which sentence is correct?|To whom should I send the letter?|To who should I send the letter?|Whom is calling?|Who did you give it to him?", "Which sentence is correct?|If I were you, I would apologize.|If I was you, I would apologize.|If I am you, I would apologize.|If I be you, I would apologize.", "Which sentence uses a dash correctly?|He had one goal — to win.|He had — one goal to win.|He — had one goal to win.|He had one goal to — win.", "What do three dots (an ellipsis) show in a quotation?|Words have been left out|The quotation is false|The speaker is shouting|A new paragraph begins", "Which sentence is correct?|Neither of the answers is right.|Neither of the answers are right.|Neither of the answers were right.|Neither of the answers be right.", "Which sentence is correct?|The data show a clear trend.|The data shows a clear trends.|The data showing a clear trend.|The data is show a clear trend.", "Which sentence uses a colon correctly?|She wanted one thing: respect.|She wanted: one thing respect.|She: wanted one thing respect.|She wanted one: thing respect.", "Which sentence is correct?|Whoever arrives first should open the door.|Whomever arrives first should open the door.|Who ever arrive first should open the door.|Whom arrives first should open the door."]);
T("Writing","Style, voice and audience","Diction, syntax and register","🖋","",
 ["Style is how a writer uses language. Diction is word choice and syntax is sentence structure.", "Register is the level of formality. Match it to your audience and purpose.", "A writer's voice is the personality that comes through the writing."],
 ["What is diction?|Word choice|Sentence length|Paragraph order|Punctuation", "What is syntax?|The way words are arranged in sentences|The meaning of a word|The title of a text|The rhyme of a poem", "What is register?|The level of formality of language|The speed of speech|The number of paragraphs|The spelling of a word", "Which register suits a university application letter?|Formal|Slang|Casual|Text-message style", "What effect do short, simple sentences often create?|Tension or urgency|Calm and delay|Confusion|Formality", "What effect do long, flowing sentences often create?|A reflective or descriptive mood|Panic|Humour only|Anger", "What do we call the personality that comes through a writer's words?|Voice|Plot|Citation|Setting", "Who should a writer think about before choosing a style?|The audience|Only themselves|The printer|Nobody", "What is the reason a text was written called?|Its purpose|Its register|Its syntax|Its diction", "Which sentence has the most formal diction?|The committee will commence its review tomorrow.|The gang's gonna get going on it tomorrow.|They'll kick things off tomorrow.|We're starting that stuff tomorrow."]);
T("Literature","Ways of reading","Critical lenses for literature","🔍","",
 ["A critical lens is a way of looking at a text. Each lens asks different questions.", "A feminist lens looks at gender and power. A Marxist lens looks at class and money. A postcolonial lens looks at empire and its effects.", "A formalist lens looks only at the text itself: its language, structure and devices."],
 ["Which lens examines how gender and power are shown?|Feminist|Marxist|Formalist|Archetypal", "Which lens examines social class and economic power?|Marxist|Feminist|Psychoanalytic|Reader-response", "Which lens examines the effects of empire and colonization?|Postcolonial|Formalist|Archetypal|Biographical", "Which lens focuses only on the text's language, structure and devices?|Formalist|Historical|Feminist|Marxist", "Which lens focuses on the unconscious mind and hidden desires of characters?|Psychoanalytic|Postcolonial|Formalist|Marxist", "Which lens says that meaning is created by each reader?|Reader-response|Formalist|Biographical|Marxist", "Which lens connects a text to the life of its author?|Biographical|Reader-response|Formalist|Archetypal", "Which lens looks for recurring patterns such as the hero's journey?|Archetypal|Marxist|Feminist|Postcolonial", "A critic asks, \"Who has money and power in this novel, and who does not?\" Which lens is this?|Marxist|Formalist|Psychoanalytic|Archetypal", "Why use more than one lens on the same text?|Each lens reveals different meanings|Only one lens is ever correct|It makes the essay longer|Lenses replace reading the text"]);
T("Literature","Narrative forms and techniques","How novels are built","📖","",
 ["Writers choose a form that suits the story. An epistolary novel is told in letters. A frame narrative is a story inside a story.", "Stream of consciousness follows a character's thoughts as they flow. In medias res means starting in the middle of the action.", "A bildungsroman follows a character growing up. A dystopia shows a frightening imagined society."],
 ["What is a novel told through letters or diary entries called?|Epistolary|Dystopian|Picaresque|Allegorical", "What is a story inside another story called?|A frame narrative|A flashback|A subplot|A prologue", "What is writing that follows a character's flowing thoughts called?|Stream of consciousness|Omniscient narration|Dialogue|Exposition", "What does \"in medias res\" mean?|Starting in the middle of the action|Starting at the very beginning|Ending without a resolution|Telling the story backward", "What is a novel about a young person growing up called?|A bildungsroman|A dystopia|A satire|A tragedy", "What is a story set in a frightening, controlled imagined society called?|A dystopia|A utopia|A fable|A pastoral", "What is a style that blends realistic settings with magical events called?|Magic realism|Naturalism|Minimalism|Gothic", "What is placing two things side by side to show contrast called?|Juxtaposition|Foreshadowing|Personification|Allusion", "What is a secondary storyline that runs beside the main plot called?|A subplot|A frame|A motif|A prologue", "What is an ending that leaves the outcome uncertain called?|An open ending|A denouement|A climax|An epilogue"]);
T("Reading","Reading: The discovery of insulin","Read the passage, then answer","💉","Answer from the passage.",
 ["Read the whole passage first.", "Find the evidence in the passage before you choose.", "Think about why the writer included the final sentence."],
 ["What was the only treatment for type 1 diabetes before the 1920s?|A starvation diet|Insulin injections|Surgery|Antibiotics", "At which university was insulin discovered?|The University of Toronto|McGill University|Oxford University|Harvard University", "Who was Charles Best at the time?|A student|A professor|A surgeon|A patient", "From which organ was the substance extracted?|The pancreas|The liver|The heart|The kidney", "What was James Collip's contribution?|He purified the extract so it was safe for humans|He was the first patient|He paid for the research|He performed the surgery", "Who was the first person to receive insulin?|Leonard Thompson|Frederick Banting|Charles Best|J. J. R. Macleod", "In what year was the Nobel Prize awarded for the discovery?|1923|1921|1922|1930", "For how much did the discoverers sell the patent?|One dollar each|One million dollars|Nothing at all|One hundred dollars each", "What does the phrase \"a death sentence\" suggest?|The disease was always fatal|Patients were sent to prison|The disease was a crime|Doctors refused to help", "What does the final sentence reveal about the discoverers?|They cared more about helping people than making money|They did not understand business|They wanted to be famous|They were forced to sell"],"","Before the 1920s, a diagnosis of type 1 diabetes was a death sentence. Doctors could only prescribe a starvation diet that added a few months of life. In the summer of 1921, at the University of Toronto, the surgeon Frederick Banting and a student, Charles Best, began experiments under the direction of Professor J. J. R. Macleod. They succeeded in extracting a substance from the pancreas that lowered blood sugar in dogs. The biochemist James Collip then purified it so that it was safe for humans. In January 1922, a fourteen-year-old boy named Leonard Thompson, who was close to death, became the first person to receive insulin. He recovered. In 1923 Banting and Macleod were awarded the Nobel Prize. The discoverers sold the patent to the university for one dollar each, because they believed the treatment should be available to everyone.");
